    "case_id": "55",
    "case_summary": "A 62-year-old woman presents with 24 hours of fever, left-sided flank pain, and foul-smelling urine. Five years earlier she underwent bilateral lung transplantation for severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (FEV1 15%). Her past medical history is notable for hypertension and a 70–pack-year smoking history. Immunosuppressive therapy consists of prednisone and tacrolimus; mycophenolate mofetil was discontinued for leukopenia. Prophylactic antimicrobial therapy includes trimethoprim–sulfamethoxazole, itraconazole, and valganciclovir (continued for detectable CMV viremia). On examination she is febrile but otherwise unrevealing. Laboratory studies show a leukocytosis of 20,000 per µL (neutrophils 18,150 per µL) and hyponatremia (122 mEq/L). Urinalysis is positive for leukocyte esterase, nitrites, and white blood cells. Blood and urine cultures grow Escherichia coli; she is initially treated with vancomycin and piperacillin–tazobactam and then de-escalated to ceftriaxone. CT of the abdomen and pelvis reveals a non-obstructing right nephrolithiasis and irregular, nodular thickening of the bladder wall, suspicious for neoplasm.",
